<section image-layout="blank" id="l9lehdc0tyg6jvr"><div class="body"><h1 align="center">2026 Market Update</h1><p align="center">Redpoint Ventures — Spring 2026</p><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>AI Capex</h4><p>~3.3% of GDP by 2030 on the bull case — rivaling railroad &amp; telecom buildouts</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Hyperscaler Spend</h4><p>$309B in 2024 → $779B by 2027E at a 36% CAGR</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Adoption Velocity</h4><p>1B+ monthly active users ~4 years post-ChatGPT vs. 70M internet users ~4 years post-Netscape</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Private Market Signal</h4><p>98% of VC funding is AI-native in Q1 2026; 24 private deals &gt;$1B vs. 7 IPOs in 2025</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="9alrey7j7aqoyls"><div class="body"><h1>We're Living Through a Genuine Technological Revolution</h1><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>Investment Scale</h4><p>AI infrastructure capex is on track to become the largest technology investment wave in US history — projected at ~3.3% of GDP by 2030 on the bull case, rivaling the railroad and telecom buildouts of prior centuries.</p><p>Hyperscaler CapEx is surging from <b>$309B in 2024</b> to an estimated <b>$779B by 2027E</b> — a compound annual growth rate of 36%. Meta leads all hyperscalers at a 54% CAGR; Google follows at 49%; Microsoft at 33%.</p></div><div><h4>This Is Not Dotcom 2.0</h4><p>The bears are wrong on the fundamentals. OpenAI and Anthropic are each generating <b>$20B+ ARR</b>. More than <b>90% of data center capacity is pre-committed</b>. Nvidia's EPS stands at ~$4.06 — versus Cisco's ~$0.37 at the peak of the 2000 bubble. The revenue is real; the commitments are real.</p><p>Adoption velocity dwarfs every prior platform shift: <b>1B+ monthly active users</b> approximately four years after ChatGPT's launch, compared with just 70M internet users four years after Netscape.</p></div></columns><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/ZE9UqtevDnVPzMUFucU2W.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="year" label="Year" type="label"></property><property key="capex" label="Hyperscaler CapEx ($B)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>2024A</cell><cell>309</cell></row><row><cell>2025E</cell><cell>498</cell></row><row><cell>2026E</cell><cell>765</cell></row><row><cell>2027E</cell><cell>779</cell></row></data></graph></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="lc1dw01ghkyosoq"><div class="body"><h1>Awe-Inspiring Products Are Shipping Weekly</h1><smart-layout variant="timeline" orientation="landscape" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Jan '25 — DeepSeek R1</h4><p>Crashed Nvidia stock ~17%. Proved frontier-quality reasoning at a fraction of the cost, reframing the compute efficiency debate.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Apr '25 — Vibe Coding</h4><p>Lovable, Replit, Cursor go mainstream. Build cost: $500K / 6 months in 2015 → <b>$50 / 6 hours in 2026</b>.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>May–Jul '25 — Science Breakthroughs</h4><p>AlphaFold 3 designs novel protein binders for new drugs. OpenAI &amp; Google beat world's top mathematicians at IMO (Gold).</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Feb '26 — Autonomy &amp; Reasoning</h4><p>Waymo operating in 10 US cities with 200M fully autonomous miles driven. Gemini 3.1 &amp; Claude 4.6 deliver agentic breakthroughs.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/N6cyUnm9MCYPZGB6C5ndH.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>Software Developer Demand: Counter-Narrative</h4><p>Despite fears of displacement, Indeed job postings for software engineers recovered to <b>97% of Jan 2024 baseline</b> by January 2026 — outpacing total job postings, which recovered to only 85%. AI is not eliminating software demand; it is reshaping it.</p></div><div><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="70" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="category" label="Category" type="label"></property><property key="index" label="Jan 2026 Index (Jan 2024 = 100)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Software Engineers</cell><cell>97</cell></row><row><cell>Total Job Postings</cell><cell>85</cell></row></data></graph></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="0b7jfnuyyyw292p"><div class="body"><h1>Agents Are Still Early — But the TAM Unlocks Are Enormous</h1><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Where We Are Today</h4><p>The industry is largely at the <b>Task Agent</b> stage: AI executes discrete tasks end-to-end with human oversight, operating with independent runtime measured in minutes. This is the beginning, not the plateau.</p><p>Coding was the first domino: GitHub code YoY growth hit ~35% in 2025; new iOS apps rose ~45% YoY; new websites up ~32% YoY. AI is <b>expanding</b> software creation, not contracting it.</p><blockquote><p>"Every engineer is effectively gonna end up being more of like a tech lead in the future that has their own little army of engineering agents." — Mark Zuckerberg</p></blockquote></div><div><h4>TAM Unlocks by Maturity Level</h4><smart-layout variant="staircase" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Copilots</h4><p>~$0.5T US software spend</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Task Agents</h4><p>~$1.2T (+$0.7T services automation)</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Workflow Agents</h4><p>~$2.8T (+$1.6T operational payroll)</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Autonomous</h4><p>~$6.1T+ (+$3.2T knowledge payroll)</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/3jnHCsDFwRjsJDrh7fitP.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="0rc5w7t098uzxex"><div class="body"><h1>Software Stocks Are Down 20% YTD — But the Selloff Is Highly Uneven</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/wWckI890ODA5f8IbTfJWF.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Sector Performance YTD 2026</h4><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="auto" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="sector" label="Sector" type="label"></property><property key="return" label="YTD Return (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Energy</cell><cell>32</cell></row><row><cell>S&amp;P 500</cell><cell>-5</cell></row><row><cell>Software</cell><cell>-20</cell></row></data></graph></div><div><h4>Within Software: A Tale of Three Markets</h4><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="auto" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="category" label="Category" type="label"></property><property key="ltm" label="LTM Return (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Vertical SaaS</cell><cell>3</cell></row><row><cell>Infrastructure</cell><cell>2</cell></row><row><cell>Horizontal SaaS</cell><cell>-35</cell></row></data></graph></div></columns><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>NTM Revenue Multiple: Near All-Time Lows</h4><p>Public SaaS median NTM revenue multiple has collapsed to <b>4.1x</b> — down from a peak of 22x in late 2020. The multiple declined to ~5x by December 2022 and has continued to compress to its current 4.1x level.</p></div><div><h4>Growth Premium Is Real</h4><p>Companies growing &gt;20% NTM trade at <b>8.1x</b> revenue. Companies growing &lt;10% NTM trade at only <b>2.9x</b>. The market is paying a clear and significant premium for durable growth. Lowest-multiple names also carry the highest daily volatility (R² = 0.50) — uncertainty is being repriced aggressively.</p></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="8c1d2btyi5w6pb0"><div class="body"><h1>Why Horizontal SaaS Is Getting Hit Hardest</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/0ZENNVBzxXnKjBKg3SmMy.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>Vertical SaaS <span text-color="#1B1B27">+3% LTM</span></h4><p>Owns irreplaceable moats — proprietary industry data, compliance workflows, and years of process history. AI can augment but cannot displace without re-acquiring that data. Switching cost is <b>existential, not cosmetic</b>.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Infrastructure +2% LTM</h4><p>AI is a <b>direct tailwind</b>. New AI workloads run on Snowflake, Datadog, MongoDB, and Cloudflare. More AI deployment = more compute, data pipeline, and observability spend. Every new model trained is a new infrastructure contract.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Horizontal SaaS −35% LTM</h4><p>Accidentally optimized for replaceability — designed to serve every industry equally, integrating deeply with none. Without proprietary data or long process history, switching is easier. These products track who does what and when — <b>exactly what AI handles well</b>.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><aside variant="warning"><p>The real concern is <b>terminal value, not near-term earnings</b>. 85–95% of SaaS enterprise value comes from terminal value. A strong quarter moves &lt;5% of value. Investors are pricing in near-zero long-term growth: a representative $100B company saw its implied terminal growth rate drop from <b>4.7% to 1.1%</b> in a single quarter, reducing market cap to $73B.</p></aside></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="czqvshf79t38pv7"><div class="body"><h1>CIOs Are Actively Consolidating Vendors — Demand &amp; Retention at Risk</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/8RlYO3ElzKLDvJYdW3cRS.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>The Consolidation Signal</h4><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item label="54%"><h4>CIOs consolidating vendors</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="45%"><h4>AI budgets replacing software</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="3%"><h4>Expect more vendors from AI</h4></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div><div><h4>Categories at Risk of Replacement</h4><p>% of CIOs seriously considering switching in the last year:</p><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="category" label="Category" type="label"></property><property key="pct" label="% CIOs Considering Switch" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Customer Service Mgmt</cell><cell>26</cell></row><row><cell>Finance Ops</cell><cell>21</cell></row><row><cell>Project Mgmt</cell><cell>20</cell></row><row><cell>Salesforce Automation</cell><cell>19</cell></row><row><cell>HRIS</cell><cell>17</cell></row></data></graph></div></columns><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Pricing Model Shift Is Underway</h4><p><b>46%</b> of CIOs expect usage/outcome-based pricing to become more common. <b>35%</b> expect flat subscriptions to decline. This is not speculation — it is current buyer expectation reshaping contract negotiations now.</p></div><div><h4>AI as the Top Spend Driver</h4><p>AI is the <b>#1 driver</b> of software spend increases: <b>58%</b> of CIOs cite AI feature additions as a key driver — the highest of any category surveyed. Being on the wrong side of this trend is existential; being on the right side is a durable growth engine.</p></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="8da70esjqwd78r3"><div class="body"><h1>AI-Native Companies Are Growing at an Unprecedented Pace</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/xFqfSG07ZT7NxE8Z4aLa-.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Revenue at Scale, Already</h4><p>OpenAI is at a <b>$25B current revenue run-rate</b> with massive projected growth through 2028E. Anthropic is at a <b>$19B current run-rate</b>. These are not future bets — they are present realities, achieved at a pace that no prior enterprise software company has matched.</p><p>AI-native companies are achieving dramatically higher ARR/FTE ratios than traditional SaaS — a structural efficiency advantage that compounds over time and makes legacy labor models uncompetitive by design.</p></div><div><h4>VC Capital Concentration</h4><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="year" label="Year" type="label"></property><property key="share" label="Top 20 Deals Share of Enterprise SW VC (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>2020</cell><cell>8</cell></row><row><cell>2021</cell><cell>6</cell></row><row><cell>2022</cell><cell>7</cell></row><row><cell>2023</cell><cell>23</cell></row><row><cell>2024</cell><cell>31</cell></row><row><cell>2025</cell><cell>44</cell></row></data></graph></div></columns><aside><p>VC funding is <b>nearly 100% AI-native</b> by Q1 2026 (98% AI vs. 2% non-AI), up from ~35% in Q1 2020. Model Labs (OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI) represent only <b>3% of deal count</b> but capture <b>67% of dollars raised</b> and <b>66% of enterprise value</b>. Capital is concentrating at the model layer with historic intensity.</p></aside></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="zn71cs5usoxiqg5"><div class="body"><h1>Private Market Valuations Are Surging — Rounds Are Closing Faster</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/252NhEbSP3Be8Tp8zxBRu.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>Valuation Step-Ups by Stage (Q1 2026)</h4><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item label="$60M"><h4>Seed median post-money</h4><p>Up from ~$20M in Q1 2024</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="$150M"><h4>Series A median</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="$480M"><h4>Series B median</h4></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="$1B"><h4>Series C median</h4></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div><div><h4>Private Is the New Public</h4><p>Late-stage private markets have effectively replaced the public markets as the venue for scale capital. In 2025: <b>24 private deals exceeded $1B</b> in size, versus only <b>7 IPOs</b>. <b>86% of US companies with $100M+ revenue are now private</b>.</p><p>Median days between fundraising rounds has fallen sharply since mid-2023, reaching multi-year lows by early 2026. The best companies are not waiting — they are pre-empting rounds and compressing timelines aggressively.</p><aside variant="success"><p><b>Historical sweet spot:</b> Companies founded 4–6 years after a major platform shift have historically been the biggest value creators. For AI, that window is <b>2026–2027</b>. We are in it now.</p></aside></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="rmv8eu2q9hoxh7x"><div class="body"><h1>What This Means for Your Company Right Now</h1><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithLabel" cell-size="15" orientation="vertical" numbered="true"><smart-layout-item><h4>Prove Your Moat Is Real</h4><p>Investors and CIOs are asking the same question: does your product own irreplaceable data, workflow, or compliance depth? If yes, quantify it. If not, articulate your switching cost clearly and credibly — or begin building that depth now before a better-positioned competitor does.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Rethink Pricing Before Your Customers Do</h4><p>Usage/outcome-based models are winning. <b>35% of CIOs</b> expect flat subscriptions to decline; <b>46%</b> expect usage/outcome pricing to grow. Get ahead of this shift proactively — companies that migrate first define the new norms; companies that wait negotiate from weakness.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>AI Is Both a Feature and a Threat</h4><p><b>58% of CIOs</b> cite AI additions as the top driver of software spend increases. But <b>45% of AI budgets</b> are replacing existing software. You must be positioned on the right side of both trends simultaneously — adding AI capability while ensuring you are not the budget being cannibalized.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Fundraising Windows Are Compressing</h4><p>Rounds are closing faster; valuations are up sharply; but capital is concentrating in fewer deals. If you are raising, move with urgency. The best terms go to the fastest movers. The top 20 deals captured <b>44% of all enterprise software VC</b> in 2025 — positioning and timing are everything.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2026–2027 Is the Historical Sweet Spot</h4><p>Every major platform shift — Internet, Mobile, Cloud — produced its biggest winners from companies founded 4–6 years in. That window is open <b>right now</b> for AI. The compounding advantage of starting in this window, with today's tools and infrastructure, will be impossible to replicate in two years.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ksz790t1qjsvxz0"><div class="body"><h1>Sources</h1><smart-layout variant="arrowBullets" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/arrow-right.svg" source="image.icon"></icon><div><p><a href="#">Redpoint Ventures — 2026 Market Update (Spring 2026)</a></p></div></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>